//! # Supervision
//!
//! A supervisor owns named child roles and restarts failed children under its
//! strategy and restart budget. Native declared-child supervision is available;
//! pools and the SimpleOneForOne path remain implementation gaps.
//!
//! A child role resolves the current incarnation on a call. A direct incarnation
//! handle remains tied to that actor; restarting a role does not revive a stale
//! incarnation. Restart reconstructs declared state, so persist application data
//! elsewhere when it must survive a child fault.
//!
//! Calls on children wait for completion, including void handlers, and return
//! actor outcomes. A missing child or exhausted restart budget must be handled as
//! failure. Do not use a sleep as proof that a replacement is ready.
//!
//! The decided shutdown forms are `close(supervisor)` to request cooperative stop
//! and wait for child cleanup, `fork close(supervisor)` to start it concurrently,
//! and `closed(supervisor)` to observe termination. Their native implementation is
//! pending; internal runtime stop functions are not public alternatives.
//!
//! See the language guide's actor and lifecycle sections and the supervision
//! contract in HEW-SPEC-2026. Source declarations alone do not establish support
//! for every supervisor strategy or target.
